\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  A three-panel collage illustrating diverse swim patterns generated by the swim CPG circuit, adapting to the maturing size of Dendronotus iris. (A) Swim CPG circuitry featuring core interneurons Si2L/R and Si3L/R, driven by the sensory interneurons Si1s. (B) Voltage traces (top) and neurotransmitter release probabilities for the four core swim CPG interneurons (SiN). (C) As {\em  Dendronotus Iris} grows from 30g to 300g over its lifespan, as shown in the voltage traces in panel\nobreakspace  {}B. its swim period slows down from 2--3s in juveniles to up to 12--14 seconds in older adults, see the voltage traces in panel\nobreakspace  {}B.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:group_fig5}{{1}{3}{A three-panel collage illustrating diverse swim patterns generated by the swim CPG circuit, adapting to the maturing size of Dendronotus iris. (A) Swim CPG circuitry featuring core interneurons Si2L/R and Si3L/R, driven by the sensory interneurons Si1s. (B) Voltage traces (top) and neurotransmitter release probabilities for the four core swim CPG interneurons (SiN). (C) As {\em Dendronotus Iris} grows from 30g to 300g over its lifespan, as shown in the voltage traces in panel~B. its swim period slows down from 2--3s in juveniles to up to 12--14 seconds in older adults, see the voltage traces in panel~B}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces  Anti-phase left–right episodes within a swim pattern, produced by the Dendronotus iris CPG under control conditions, arise from twisted pairwise modules (Si3R $\leftrightarrows $ Si2L and Si3L $\leftrightarrows $ Si2R) that are unidirectionally coupled via excitatory ($\blacktriangledown $) and inhibitory ($\bullet $) synapses. These modules alternate in initiating the onset of network bursting, an effect further reinforced by two reciprocally inhibitory HSOs, consisting respectively of Si3R–Si3L and Si2R–Si2L pairs. Compare with the original Fig.1\nobreakspace  {}from the experimental study\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {sakurai2022bursting}. }}{3}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:group_fig5}{{2}{3}{Anti-phase left–right episodes within a swim pattern, produced by the Dendronotus iris CPG under control conditions, arise from twisted pairwise modules (Si3R $\leftrightarrows $ Si2L and Si3L $\leftrightarrows $ Si2R) that are unidirectionally coupled via excitatory ($\blacktriangledown $) and inhibitory ($\bullet $) synapses. These modules alternate in initiating the onset of network bursting, an effect further reinforced by two reciprocally inhibitory HSOs, consisting respectively of Si3R–Si3L and Si2R–Si2L pairs. Compare with the original Fig.1~from the experimental study~\cite {sakurai2022bursting}}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Comparison of specific, distinct responses of the swim CPG to targeted perturbations, produced by injecting hyperpolarizing and depolarizing current pulses (red vertical arrows) into selected interneurons. This comparison validates the CPG model against its biological counterpart and demonstrates the resilience of both systems in restoring and maintaining normal swim patterns. Fact-check list\nobreakspace  {}I: Compare these six modeling panels with their corresponding experimental originals in Figs. 5A–C and E–G of Ref. \cite  {SakuraiKatz2016}. For empirical evidence of resilience, see Fig.\nobreakspace  {}5 in Ref. \cite  {SakuraiKatz2017}. For the PIR proof, see Fig. 5 in the other experimental Ref.\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {sakurai2022bursting}.}}{4}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:group_fig5}{{3}{4}{Comparison of specific, distinct responses of the swim CPG to targeted perturbations, produced by injecting hyperpolarizing and depolarizing current pulses (red vertical arrows) into selected interneurons. This comparison validates the CPG model against its biological counterpart and demonstrates the resilience of both systems in restoring and maintaining normal swim patterns. Fact-check list~I: Compare these six modeling panels with their corresponding experimental originals in Figs. 5A–C and E–G of Ref. \cite {SakuraiKatz2016}. For empirical evidence of resilience, see Fig.~5 in Ref. \cite {SakuraiKatz2017}. For the PIR proof, see Fig. 5 in the other experimental Ref.~\cite {sakurai2022bursting}}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Similar responses of the swim CPG during control swims to various temporal perturbations—produced by applying hyperpolarizing or depolarizing current pulses (red vertical arrows) to targeted interneurons—allow us to validate the CPG model against its biological counterparts. Fact-check list\nobreakspace  {}II: compare the four modeling panels with their corresponding experimental originals shown in Figs. 7Ai–-Di of Ref.\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {SakuraiKatz2016}. }}{4}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:group_fig7}{{4}{4}{Similar responses of the swim CPG during control swims to various temporal perturbations—produced by applying hyperpolarizing or depolarizing current pulses (red vertical arrows) to targeted interneurons—allow us to validate the CPG model against its biological counterparts. Fact-check list~II: compare the four modeling panels with their corresponding experimental originals shown in Figs. 7Ai–-Di of Ref.~\cite {SakuraiKatz2016}}{figure.4}{}}
\citation{RL87}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Interneuron Si1 excites Si3 neurons, thereby initiating and terminating the entire rhythmically bursting CPG (in the absence of neuromodulation). Fact-check list\nobreakspace  {}II to compare: i) Panel A with its experimental original — Fig. 2A from Ref. \cite  {sakurai2019command}; ii) Panels B and C with their corresponding originals — Figs. 3A and 3B from Ref. \cite  {sakurai2019command}, respectively.}}{5}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:group_fig5}{{5}{5}{Interneuron Si1 excites Si3 neurons, thereby initiating and terminating the entire rhythmically bursting CPG (in the absence of neuromodulation). Fact-check list~II to compare: i) Panel A with its experimental original — Fig. 2A from Ref. \cite {sakurai2019command}; ii) Panels B and C with their corresponding originals — Figs. 3A and 3B from Ref. \cite {sakurai2019command}, respectively}{figure.5}{}}
