\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Fact check IV: Compare the panels B and C with the original neurophysiological data — Fig. 7 in Ref. \cite  {sakurai2022bursting}. Three positive current pulses of increasing amplitude were applied to Si2R to calibrate the $\alpha $-synapse and logistic synapse models (bottom trace), as well as the IPSP responses in the contralateral Si2L (top trace). Two positive current pulses injected into Si2R triggered accumulating IPSP responses in Si2L (top trace) and elicited fast and slow echoes in Si3L. These echoes arise from the fast gap junction and delayed slow inhibition mediated by the logistic synapse between Si2R and Si3L, respectively (see the swim CPG circuitry in Fig.\nobreakspace  {}1).}}{6}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:group_fig5}{{6}{6}{Fact check IV: Compare the panels B and C with the original neurophysiological data — Fig. 7 in Ref. \cite {sakurai2022bursting}. Three positive current pulses of increasing amplitude were applied to Si2R to calibrate the $\alpha $-synapse and logistic synapse models (bottom trace), as well as the IPSP responses in the contralateral Si2L (top trace). Two positive current pulses injected into Si2R triggered accumulating IPSP responses in Si2L (top trace) and elicited fast and slow echoes in Si3L. These echoes arise from the fast gap junction and delayed slow inhibition mediated by the logistic synapse between Si2R and Si3L, respectively (see the swim CPG circuitry in Fig.~1)}{figure.6}{}}
